[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad01848-8741-5cb8-87a5-f6517290d6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342468} {:answer/id #uuid "71c169d6-5705-5a09-9d3e-2b9fa9418633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343220} {:answer/id #uuid "9a17635b-0972-57cb-b31e-499ad5931fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343594} {:answer/id #uuid "c7525d1b-d02f-5b0f-bcd5-f1d6c6826b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343988} {:answer/id #uuid "f91bc710-bf1b-51b9-8455-71a367e23015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad01848-8741-5cb8-87a5-f6517290d6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342468} :answer-field/id #uuid "af5f01ce-9357-53cf-ac88-d236cd72d59a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af6a3602-6598-5ad6-8883-59a5609544bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75302" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ),4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4(0),4(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d86c569-1b23-58e1-8325-4f3e7e75a69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342353} {:answer/id #uuid "225011dd-679b-5c4b-b454-3b0b1271da1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342522} {:answer/id #uuid "60fd3304-972d-5e5d-9fb2-f58c18df414c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343059} {:answer/id #uuid "8b30390b-535a-5fbf-a016-d26514ebf39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343449} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0dcad5-ba23-558d-b9a1-58b76db4a021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf0dcad5-ba23-558d-b9a1-58b76db4a021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343917} :answer-field/id #uuid "818560be-6c53-5bbb-8884-04841718675f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e872eb2a-80c0-5fe7-b915-1fedf1110b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-75407" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(1)=-i+j+\\frac{1}{2}k,\\,{q}^{′}(1)=j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{4(1)}{({1}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(1)+\\frac{2}{{1}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}(-i+j+\\frac{1}{2}k)+\\frac{2}{3}(j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\,j+\\frac{4}{9}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{2}+{(\\frac{2}{9})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{81}+\\frac{4}{81}+\\frac{16}{81}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{4}{9}\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\,j+\\frac{4}{9}\\,k}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{2}{3}q(1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-i+j+\\frac{1}{2}k)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i\\cdot \\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j\\cdot j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k\\cdot k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14477158-11de-5790-b879-bc43c8ca8958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342417} {:answer/id #uuid "2441c22a-b9d8-5652-ae6e-8b99dc23bcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342545} {:answer/id #uuid "364edc20-5721-5b95-b18f-7e2fcb0c7c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342686} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5c501c-a1fa-5e2e-8a3d-da88a08e2ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343745} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfae4a4-bf96-5ad0-813a-3a5b34415980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdfae4a4-bf96-5ad0-813a-3a5b34415980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343911} :answer-field/id #uuid "49407303-e12b-5aac-a9e2-6cef8ca783a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a57d2de-dfa3-5922-be9d-d7417adc9dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75448" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c81bfce-9632-5e30-aca0-66bfd54ed5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342869} {:answer/id #uuid "6696bf37-f7ff-50df-9318-a659fdf44683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343107} {:answer/id #uuid "669b4061-b6a6-5c32-bcc9-77f78daffdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343108} {:answer/id #uuid "991af7f3-b4f6-515e-a7e8-d7d5a51f6ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343584} {:answer/id #uuid "c959123c-a4c9-5b03-b0d7-7a0e84a3f053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669b4061-b6a6-5c32-bcc9-77f78daffdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343108} :answer-field/id #uuid "619bf93e-94cf-5c5d-8b42-901d98684e52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c985bafb-2593-5b73-b834-81a6aec3295e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75458" :question/problem "Find ${F}^{′}(e)$ if $F(x)={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{a}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{u}{2+{u}^{2}})+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}})+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2x+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${F}^{′}(e).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}{2e+e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2e+e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "375ef518-4d6a-56b5-8701-cd9802686411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342695} {:answer/id #uuid "41e5f4d8-856b-52f5-85b3-07ac865cef2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342784} {:answer/id #uuid "69d5fca1-eec8-5d54-b1fc-d0f9751e2efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343141} {:answer/id #uuid "745b8d3b-d175-5c42-a346-9db9b829c2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343245} {:answer/id #uuid "af1ce442-73ac-5780-9670-756b226a0a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af1ce442-73ac-5780-9670-756b226a0a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343779} :answer-field/id #uuid "74402250-c07b-5643-a659-e21ce9c35c06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b15f4c73-c376-5fbd-b671-915cb2fd0f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75491" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,0,-4⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c234f6-092e-5f46-865a-425945404213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343206} {:answer/id #uuid "72377fdc-c5c4-5cad-be35-e357b4b262ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343227} {:answer/id #uuid "8be39f08-c60d-56cb-867b-ae591af34e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343461} {:answer/id #uuid "9f960098-4e3f-597c-a84f-8d0dfb1daa4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343646} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8ce6f6-003a-5a0f-82e6-f33b9254bcdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72377fdc-c5c4-5cad-be35-e357b4b262ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343227} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1ca2441-2869-5b83-a4db-5247f47eb190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a9495b4-c709-53eb-875a-846142be09cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-75521" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j]\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=\\pm 1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)=1\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j=\\,i+j, \\\\ r(-1)=(-1)\\,i+\\frac{1}{(-1)}\\,j=-\\,i-j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0191e885-4eaa-5810-988a-c30eb71a3ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342262} {:answer/id #uuid "056a40fc-c58d-5c4c-ab62-664c240b4261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342290} {:answer/id #uuid "33643afa-ac2e-5b9f-adc3-b2783e20d066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342671} {:answer/id #uuid "35947d12-1855-548c-b110-dedcae0828e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342679} {:answer/id #uuid "83eb6ca0-8566-5ea4-a7c4-84d928c8be41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33643afa-ac2e-5b9f-adc3-b2783e20d066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342671} :answer-field/id #uuid "b899eb10-9d4b-51ba-91ba-e034d3dbe1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ed3b4a4-4334-5910-b956-ca1595cafddd" :question/math-academy-id "q-75524" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,t\\in [0,\\pi )$ find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in [0,\\pi )$, the only solutions are $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨0\\,,\\,3⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨1\\,,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(0,3)$ and $(1,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241e4e41-3877-590f-b9fb-f9441f5491c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342541} {:answer/id #uuid "2a868b11-a8ff-5adf-ab9a-9bf43adf6226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342591} {:answer/id #uuid "8689e7c8-b5a6-587c-8850-666b874ed56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343396} {:answer/id #uuid "d624b7cc-f612-5da1-90f6-60de0f22b6c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{8}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344113} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7baec1-72af-559b-b4c9-4860c48320be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8689e7c8-b5a6-587c-8850-666b874ed56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343396} :answer-field/id #uuid "db324572-0ff6-5834-9630-8030d14c66f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36f60655-da6b-51ba-a53b-de029dead882" :question/math-academy-id "q-75828" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+16x+3=(5x+1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3} & =\\frac{14}{(5x+1)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(5x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+3)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n14=A(x+3)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-3+3)+B(5(-3)+1) \\\\ 14 & =-14B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}+3)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{14}{5}A \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}=\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|{|}_{4}^{11} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "703429e4-f6ee-5ae0-b54b-621a3b3aa87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,y=-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343202} {:answer/id #uuid "a374971b-c760-5d83-adbf-3b1936e47374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343673} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd32382-778d-546c-b434-bc2f6f15182c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343889} {:answer/id #uuid "bd80722c-1778-5d31-a005-82a0a1a70cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343905} {:answer/id #uuid "f93e23d5-adfb-539c-932c-d93d3f7f5111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd80722c-1778-5d31-a005-82a0a1a70cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343905} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e62a385-bb95-5754-89d7-c5b01db0db60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "263b5643-f9a7-58b5-b4f2-be4d8aef81b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75862" :question/problem "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y &=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y &=2 && |\\times 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y &=1 && |\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-y &=6 \\\\ 2x-y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $y$ term that can cancel with the $-y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-y &=6 &&  \\\\ 2x-y &=4 && |\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-y &=6 \\\\ -2x+y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-\\cancel{y} & =6 \\\\ -2x+\\cancel{y} & =-4 \\\\ (6x-2x) & =(6-4) \\\\ 4x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equation to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =4 \\\\ 2(\\frac{1}{2})-y & =4 \\\\ 1-y & =4 \\\\ y & =1-4 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2685fdf5-b2ef-5419-a715-1c74ce9389ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342565} {:answer/id #uuid "26c73ff8-7bb1-58f5-b9d4-61756b11ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342568} {:answer/id #uuid "75251e7a-9236-5e4c-9b53-7cfd86320732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343251} {:answer/id #uuid "978d082a-57ab-588e-8901-810af078103c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343568} {:answer/id #uuid "9f71a6a2-9c4d-582e-afe9-1afe12b68ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26c73ff8-7bb1-58f5-b9d4-61756b11ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342568} :answer-field/id #uuid "4171e3f8-5f6b-53b2-9b4c-3f49129ae323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd8936d7-9acb-5f42-aac4-919b57567005" :question/math-academy-id "q-75925" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x-2}{5x}=\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $5x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{5x} & =\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5 \\\\ 5x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{5x}) & =5x\\cdot (\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5) \\\\ 5x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{5x} & =5x\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5x\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{5x}} & =\\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{\\cancel{5x}}+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3-20x+25x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =3-20x+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3+5x \\\\ 5x-x & =-2-3 \\\\ 4x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{5}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "291c8e4e-9d45-5531-b4d1-293184452bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342584} {:answer/id #uuid "86c25b24-aec5-5597-aa76-dbce75d2dbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343400} {:answer/id #uuid "9606c201-763a-5f66-8e5e-0d490263e373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343550} {:answer/id #uuid "c49f4552-deab-57bc-bafc-6979cd09a794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343968} {:answer/id #uuid "d6136878-73ad-5b7b-992b-be1d04476b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c49f4552-deab-57bc-bafc-6979cd09a794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343968} :answer-field/id #uuid "d21d17cd-7a08-5ec9-a91a-29252aa6e304" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18e01818-579e-59cf-ab50-d0abd274fe70" :question/math-academy-id "q-75930" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $\\frac{a+3}{3a}=\\frac{a-1}{a}+2.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $a=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $a$ and $3a.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(a,3a)=3a.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3a,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a+3}{3a} & =\\frac{a-1}{a}+2 \\\\ 3a\\cdot (\\frac{a+3}{3a}) & =3a\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{a}+2) \\\\ 3a\\cdot \\frac{a+3}{3a} & =3a\\cdot \\frac{a-1}{a}+3a\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{3a}\\cdot \\frac{a+3}{\\cancel{3a}} & =3\\cancel{a}\\cdot \\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{a}}+6a \\\\ a+3 & =3(a-1)+6a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+3 & =3(a-1)+6a \\\\ a+3 & =9a-3 \\\\ 9a-a & =3+3 \\\\ 8a & =6 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. But since $a=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a7452c-e3f9-526d-82be-805b605b288f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342420} {:answer/id #uuid "2377a0c3-c1bd-53c7-93bf-77cbc38fa76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342532} {:answer/id #uuid "7c304e6a-4939-558c-9504-44c7f57d0bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343307} {:answer/id #uuid "b825111c-c17e-5e28-b1d7-789b226b6fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343852} {:answer/id #uuid "cef19a74-15fe-5db4-b7f1-7bf698a07ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b825111c-c17e-5e28-b1d7-789b226b6fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343852} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9331d1-6e4e-5569-b803-cfc624eda5cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64167e5a-a3e2-55d1-956c-8945cc13aa71" :question/math-academy-id "q-75941" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}=\\frac{m+4}{15m}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3m,5m,$ and $15m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3m,5m,15m)=15m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $15m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m} & =\\frac{m+4}{15m} \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+3\\cdot 5m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{\\cancel{3m}})+3\\cdot \\cancel{5m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{\\cancel{5m}}) & =\\cancel{15m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{\\cancel{15m}}) \\\\ 5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4 \\\\ 10m-15+3m+15 & =m+4 \\\\ 13m & =m+4 \\\\ 12m & =4 \\\\ m & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68d0aa0d-d15b-53b1-b587-29d21d242662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343134} {:answer/id #uuid "8454df55-5625-586c-8f0c-e59a199cf2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343370} {:answer/id #uuid "897525de-2820-5f27-9f87-c7818129d1a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343431} {:answer/id #uuid "ace01dcb-3f03-5590-92b8-6bbe03249e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343761} {:answer/id #uuid "c4178081-bdf7-5733-b5be-9444a4cfd6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68d0aa0d-d15b-53b1-b587-29d21d242662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343134} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf34ce4-972f-542d-98a2-41b9843bb493" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29f3d15c-3699-5650-acaa-cf842bca8086" :question/math-academy-id "q-75944" :question/problem "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${d}_{3}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $D$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{8}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is ${{d}_{1},{d}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}}_{{d}_{3}}=\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{d}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(2)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}_{{d}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 2=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3214ac66-089e-5f1a-b5f5-27127d89af81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342662} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6f8a38-0a5a-5b8d-9bbd-8d6b55a7767b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343032} {:answer/id #uuid "69df0ecf-34a7-5ab2-a85a-47fd55e5f70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343142} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9fef81-c774-530d-bce3-3ec15ebb79e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344469} {:answer/id #uuid "f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423617} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab78a2e2-73c6-516c-bb41-33e676435ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c466a2ff-d594-54f8-8655-5aeb347fd10d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75980" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${v}_{2}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c192211-a062-5d76-8e3f-cac4eb8e50eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342476} {:answer/id #uuid "2b04914a-76f1-51a8-a7c9-b2a4d26db2e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342597} {:answer/id #uuid "b72eebd1-ed24-5a3c-9407-3e4f89404335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343845} {:answer/id #uuid "cd05bdbf-6440-5186-86f9-6868a4471153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344045} {:answer/id #uuid "d38031bb-76d7-5bef-b74e-24bfabbc3646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd05bdbf-6440-5186-86f9-6868a4471153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344045} :answer-field/id #uuid "96deaadc-3e11-5c45-b3ef-17e9f80f5c36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a0673ad-ec47-547a-a169-9262732d993a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75987" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x+1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23632137-2307-53ed-97ca-6d194aedb4b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342531} {:answer/id #uuid "37d31442-bc77-52e8-8b42-ed69f127f1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342699} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba06784-f60e-51b7-a033-405a8dce8d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342727} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdcc48e-a176-50a3-b60b-3fba9a585a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342738} {:answer/id #uuid "db10b79e-7bf8-5af2-b77f-58f018741190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db10b79e-7bf8-5af2-b77f-58f018741190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344164} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb274ae8-518d-5d65-b604-82ec570fbbc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df30d89a-58a8-55cb-b4e5-f7285000fdf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76070" :question/problem "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3x-12}{{x}^{2}+1}).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function is a logarithmic function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3x-12}{{x}^{2}+1}),$ we set the expression under the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-12}{{x}^{2}+1}>0\\,⟹\\,3x-12>0\\,⟹\\,x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3x-12}{{x}^{2}+1})$ is $(4,\\infty ),$ and it is also continuous on this interval."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d37236-cdb6-53ee-ae81-dc11f5d0e79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342779} {:answer/id #uuid "4dadc677-4324-5cdf-b675-729c97ce84df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)" :db/