is $(2,\\infty ),$ and it is also continuous on this interval."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a8011c1-9cfc-5fa1-bbdd-091c67e18332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342719} {:answer/id #uuid "63cef5b5-d373-5b1f-8528-cc13e3026246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343086} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc3db4c-1ba1-5e07-a179-70559ebd7afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343169} {:answer/id #uuid "725e4d8f-30f3-541a-b361-47b9d40fc3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343229} {:answer/id #uuid "d7be1e3b-a646-5934-9f54-a7f5ec33461f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725e4d8f-30f3-541a-b361-47b9d40fc3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343229} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b10f5e9-21ca-540c-a98f-057955c52343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c47b28a-2993-50a5-9de3-ebf5581e87c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-76106" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x-1} & =3 \\\\ 2x-1 & =9 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 5-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188201ef-f62b-54fe-91ea-ab9262b8bb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342454} {:answer/id #uuid "2012c3e8-acfe-5689-ba9a-41ed1fb940e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342501} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbbdc6b-3da7-534d-ad34-7a670c808ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343180} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f0e26d-cba8-5c20-bd03-964668ddac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343974} {:answer/id #uuid "cb94719a-6026-5bb2-956b-94cdf8043975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb94719a-6026-5bb2-956b-94cdf8043975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344030} :answer-field/id #uuid "9acaee00-2ee9-5648-9ed0-2f7eb59f1242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "373bfa7c-f3cf-5be4-9a31-c2d8cf824718" :question/math-academy-id "q-76255" :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 3\\text{i} & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, find the result of applying to $A$ the following elementary row operation:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3\\text{i}}{2-\\text{i}}){R}_{1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the row operations to $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{+}1 \\\\ 3\\text{i} & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3\\text{i}}{2-\\text{i}}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 3\\text{i}+(-\\frac{3\\text{i}(2-\\text{i})}{2-\\text{i}}) & -2+(-\\frac{3\\text{i}(2\\text{i}+1)}{2-\\text{i}})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{(-4+2\\text{i})+(6-3\\text{i})}{2-\\text{i}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the result  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} & 2\\text{i}+1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2deb5ecd-9fcb-5de8-a47f-2aac32b50a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{2},2)$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342625} {:answer/id #uuid "4f75afc8-c8e2-552b-bf47-67f0c0a13567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342897} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd46a16-dd73-5cd7-848b-a231cc8b9c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343050} {:answer/id #uuid "6734105b-9de9-5be8-a53a-4158d98ddacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{2},-2)$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343113} {:answer/id #uuid "f715325d-e759-56c1-bf0d-6cd6e0eee098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},-2)$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fd46a16-dd73-5cd7-848b-a231cc8b9c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$, radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343050} :answer-field/id #uuid "58052dca-b02a-538c-bcb6-2bc7ad90fc13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9155ce6a-a592-5aef-9c65-bf69cca03a69" :question/math-academy-id "q-76369" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}-x+{y}^{2}-4y=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{y}^{2}-4y & =-2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}) & =-2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}) & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4b4a86-f169-5c4f-87f5-d0b62ec60298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342323} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce40828-670c-5edd-87b2-c0cb375a82f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342739} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed3b169-f0d4-5213-afa6-342dddae141a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342758} {:answer/id #uuid "92eceef3-679c-5de1-b434-dd837f79c2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343524} {:answer/id #uuid "d14e25aa-c14c-530e-b787-28f9bb6a6b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ed3b169-f0d4-5213-afa6-342dddae141a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342758} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2b3b34-415d-5dcc-bc53-182c2750c2e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "385da02b-d681-50b2-a172-67bb1e6a6af1" :question/math-academy-id "q-76370" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+3x+{y}^{2}-y=-\\frac{9}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x+{y}^{2}-y & =-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+3x)+({y}^{2}-y) & =-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-\\frac{9}{4}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-(-\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and its radius is $\\frac{1}{2}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "372a5048-17c2-5076-9635-4ff4ad531da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}+2t-1)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342693} {:answer/id #uuid "60710012-7ccb-54d9-aac8-7c21a4db39de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343056} {:answer/id #uuid "7143c54a-f0e9-502c-b646-975daaf14a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343209} {:answer/id #uuid "77d755de-6003-5532-a85f-2d9e8cb137d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}-2t)\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343274} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8c1d1a-5321-50f7-aff8-7b81e9c745ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}+2t)\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60710012-7ccb-54d9-aac8-7c21a4db39de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343056} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d47b24e-5186-5a2c-bc62-0a4991b4b99e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e477990a-d15e-595b-a078-39c7382f9c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-76377" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $f(x,y,z)=2x-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1,\\,t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1,\\,t+1⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2},$ $y=t-1,$ and $z=t+1.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=2x-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({t}^{2})-(t-1)^{2}-(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-({t}^{2}-2t+1)-({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}+2t-1-{t}^{2}-2t-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{2(2{t}^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b986f99-dbf2-5721-9c5f-e28e10f22836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342338} {:answer/id #uuid "8b32fc34-f436-5a96-bd40-dfbb2b02b7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343451} {:answer/id #uuid "986babd3-8642-5f38-b06b-f77cd25a6e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343576} {:answer/id #uuid "c61d6ac5-d343-5721-8ec3-ef2a1c75053a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343976} {:answer/id #uuid "cebfa446-9404-5449-ade5-0f39864d83f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "986babd3-8642-5f38-b06b-f77cd25a6e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343576} :answer-field/id #uuid "6acd1e7a-f9b0-54b4-b6b0-e3a07466b614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4796dc75-cdb4-53f9-8768-a4a7b1757c4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76401" :question/problem "Express $3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n3({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}))\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4df8c281-7720-5de4-bfcb-7ec3f40cd92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+2){e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342878} {:answer/id #uuid "8964b90d-0fb7-5352-a378-5854b735da72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{5t+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343429} {:answer/id #uuid "90b58700-1443-53b7-900c-fe295b8988e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343503} {:answer/id #uuid "92c59c13-5a91-5d60-9e87-defff709bc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343520} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e0ad1c-9242-5e5e-8095-61f3d7da1e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\sqrt{26t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90b58700-1443-53b7-900c-fe295b8988e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343503} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f4fe4ae-1db5-5267-909a-483c3e60cca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d30373c-bec9-57f2-8b55-adb3bc1e9678" :question/math-academy-id "q-76414" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=5x+2$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=5t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,5t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+2)j \\\\  & =i+5\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(5t+2){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0470023d-2f7c-572b-8223-a821e9591b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342286} {:answer/id #uuid "21e4ce2b-359a-5d4a-b474-cf8acda842ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342519} {:answer/id #uuid "3109d7d4-9f83-52e3-8ef2-13bdd11c2459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342650} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d1ba27-795d-5ca7-bff5-0a3d75d5b66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343667} {:answer/id #uuid "d10bbab9-4de9-5d18-a90a-7d251ca7ad2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0470023d-2f7c-572b-8223-a821e9591b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342286} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3ec0e9-f2ac-56af-9dc2-ed440ea409ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2497006d-58cf-53a4-9f8c-593f6d881048" :question/math-academy-id "q-76431" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4\\sqrt{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 4\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{t}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})}{t}=\\frac{t}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{t}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{25}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}{|}_{1}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({25}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(125-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2157cbec-dd3c-50c6-8f66-e922ba1f66ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342514} {:answer/id #uuid "46847ff5-553b-5f26-9805-09a27496f196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342819} {:answer/id #uuid "52b6665a-0ccb-5b28-87e4-9cb8ee2700ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi -1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342928} {:answer/id #uuid "a88c1662-aadb-5062-a115-55611ab6c159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343722} {:answer/id #uuid "bfef22ae-f97c-5172-89a1-1bc5619b881d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46847ff5-553b-5f26-9805-09a27496f196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342819} :answer-field/id #uuid "09fe84d2-3042-5159-a1de-85adb6692d1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5649582e-ddb6-5db4-a7c3-699a007eaafe" :question/math-academy-id "q-76433" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7aeab2-63a6-55b6-9670-af11f7878d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342743} {:answer/id #uuid "55aaa22c-0a54-5244-a488-f06f0ea67eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342950} {:answer/id #uuid "7df5078f-cc9c-5640-bdbe-c73ad5edaf2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343323} {:answer/id #uuid "d44736e7-181c-5708-8fc6-091f82bf604a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344100} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc86749-0a58-547d-b292-dd0c15c33cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55aaa22c-0a54-5244-a488-f06f0ea67eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342950} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbabc5e-406f-59f6-a3fe-8b87b2e1d083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7a51cb6-cac0-57a7-bccc-fcb3f00d4af4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76434" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=t-4.$     \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4)j \\\\  & =i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(4-t)}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}t\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot (4-0) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "330a0610-7510-562c-a947-1ed175f73e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342669} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec7f114-71dd-5707-a837-49ec4e0f58d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343037} {:answer/id #uuid "784dc8af-a9a4-53e0-8947-d481e7980aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343277} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5af1a3-65b7-5d58-b8b9-b68cc63abf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343333} {:answer/id #uuid "e3af6d11-71f3-548a-854f-ff88ce98cd06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "330a0610-7510-562c-a947-1ed175f73e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342669} :answer-field/id #uuid "b827e50e-d12a-5657-bed2-ba45d90ca8cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6c30ef5-4b00-5501-a5b6-18892cf4af06" :question/math-academy-id "q-76447" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=4{t}^{2}$ and $y=3{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})j \\\\  & =8t\\,i+6t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8t)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{t}^{2}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =10|t| \\\\  & =10t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t>0$ along the path of $C.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-2{y}^{3}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={(4{t}^{2})}^{3}-2{(3{t}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}{t}^{6}-2\\cdot {3}^{3}{t}^{6} \\\\  & =64{t}^{6}-54{t}^{6} \\\\  & =10{t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{t}^{6}\\cdot 10t\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =100{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{7}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{8}}{8}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =100[\\frac{{1}^{8}}{8}+\\frac{{0}^{8}}{8}] \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =12.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec86e50-8126-5c8c-9c9e-7b518ae7992e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342364} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8f2e2d-51c7-5375-9e3c-dedb16da721e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343311} {:answer/id #uuid "a735d400-c4c3-527f-96ac-bc42ae20a5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343710} {:answer/id #uuid "adb27abf-79df-56ff-bf2b-b4946023e074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343766} {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb48e-7c9d-51a8-92b1-9e4b43f6a2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb48e-7c9d-51a8-92b1-9e4b43f6a2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343969} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75af4f4-1064-571f-8598-a6fa76be3720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d73cf87-1a19-5cca-88b7-764818d04cb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76479" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0<t<\\sqrt{\\pi /2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=-{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,j-2t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}|t| \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ because $t>0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d872230-dffa-5440-9150-e6a7484563c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{2}-6\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342745} {:answer/id #uuid "41027de0-afa8-5c2b-8896-f3199a916c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342773} {:answer/id #uuid "679fdec6-f7e1-5541-a399-fb14f4197dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343120} {:answer/id #uuid "970fe583-040b-5004-90dd-58e93faa89d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(16\\sqrt{2}-6\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343562} {:answer/id #uuid "bad2408b-9fca-5a3a-8105-a9d53cef466b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679fdec6-f7e1-5541-a399-fb14f4197dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343120} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5d8aa7-e05a-504e-8119-e3b3b13e8c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d9906db-a501-54d2-bbd1-292e69086032" :question/math-academy-id "q-76480" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ $\\,1\\le x\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{1}{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}}{{t}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}3{x}^{5}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}$, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{3/2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{4}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4b14bf-da94-5587-9476-c6c9f1763513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342466} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd66d9f-895b-589b-a593-4fcc58493314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342486} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee36c09-cb9f-5764-9a87-cf5b7c3eff99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342494} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3d5513-4913-5c07-b97e-b502cd923def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342600} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5cec4d-eafc-5470-8930-282ed398fa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ee36c09-cb9f-5764-9a87-cf5b7c3eff99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342494} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e475247-06f5-5122-b5e7-edfc19026889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ffd2389-f864-5810-9cd3-9a41b1f9dc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-76484" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5},$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$   \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =5|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t| \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\ge 0$ when $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{sin}^{2}⁡t}{5} \\\\  & =5{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =25{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =25\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =25(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6045acb1-8b9a-5676-9cd0-f476e917695e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343055} {:answer/id #uuid "78f375e1-b70a-5ed9-91e3-487c68d7b979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343283} {:answer/id #uuid "9a216f95-d7dd-56ff-a663-b17e7986e503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343595} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ff0d93-7c8a-5709-b129-b090117e05ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344250} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3a2e8b-d768-5657-967c-eae45b12f823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78f375e1-b70a-5ed9-91e3-487c68d7b979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343283} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a04b20-97d2-5960-810a-402a92da8b2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc662357-bb5a-5c14-b4f2-075042070ad7" :question/math-academy-id "q-76488" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={150}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${150}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={150}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/d6/d61c6dbd94be14f2a455791a584a00f19ab7b52452b3acda984c5b278a5e39ed.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={150}^{∘},{210}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=150\\cdot 210=31\\,500.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b458ab9-f8d2-5847-a1d7-a82898766471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342334} {:answer/id #uuid "20197b9b-a175-536e-b070-6b0342f0432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342502} {:answer/id #uuid "4e63fcf2-15c2-57a2-9183-f2e3d764402c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342883} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5024a8-ad7b-5afe-b439-92410b5aeb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343012} {:answer/id #uuid "7639ae17-8091-53b1-8ba7-0966fa92e843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b458ab9-f8d2-5847-a1d7-a82898766471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342334} :answer-field/id #uuid "78baf57b-c872-5926-9e6f-da7c5cccc697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20b45040-332b-5b0d-9817-efef67df2755" :question/math-academy-id "q-76490" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=6$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})=1.403\\,348…≈1.403\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.403\\,3$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.403\\,3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.403\\,3.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.403\\,3.$\n\n![](images/9b/9b3d60be42ec916f0e62f3a86cb1ce2aa628482c97f68f0df7c6ab533b919102.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.403\\,3 \\\\  & ≈4.879\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.403\\,3\\,\\cdot 4.879\\,9≈6.8\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b563221-3548-560c-9fc8-b290090582fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342472} {:answer/id #uuid "34a3d682-f094-5b8e-9966-be8b9fc8ad8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342675} {:answer/id #uuid "618e4548-1271-521c-9a14-4e3337de95f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343065} {:answer/id #uuid "cd843b1d-6720-54f4-98b0-8e4c42afe883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344048} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f98bc6-232d-5249-b4a6-3f2a6e7f4860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4f98bc6-232d-5249-b4a6-3f2a6e7f4860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344249} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf920228-2dc2-55b3-a8f5-efbc9659d941" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "004b67de-a618-52fc-b8e0-1a45d41d2be5" :question/math-academy-id "q-76496" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k$\nfor $1<t<\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=t,$ $y=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},$ and $z=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+t\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}}{t}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+2{t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=4t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({5}^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "629598a2-bf76-5692-87df-54a7aebab167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343074} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac07520-bae0-5d27-a68e-d13a143ff9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343446} {:answer/id #uuid "adbfbb60-3559-5add-b48d-7ec204e4d217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343767} {:answer/id #uuid "be04fed9-e037-5f1b-9c74-a5caaef8e7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343912} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bf0071-88d4-5078-a1a8-093a1a23e04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "629598a2-bf76-5692-87df-54a7aebab167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343074} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdbb8fb-f459-54a9-a836-e4514336c164" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d978c8fd-7c4e-51e3-a8f6-ce9bb51f0bbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-76499" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get the first solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/af/afb20de50d2fa92c6550d1e5357b2aaf07311177e3ed82e1a15facccf2c0f0db.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value does not lie in the required domain. To get the solution that's within the given domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6},-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b4c92cd-b35d-5c18-a223-49150a03011a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\sqrt{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342724} {:answer/id #uuid "9854cc39-fd63-5ffa-8334-c3108e197497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343574} {:answer/id #uuid "a719e5db-f272-5b05-bc3d-faa57d00c911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343708} {:answer/id #uuid "ac56b14e-40b3-5364-ac94-8f30ec9d2137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343754} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f2338d-321a-5c08-ba22-d59d140ae37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9854cc39-fd63-5ffa-8334-c3108e197497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343574} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe61a69d-0a4f-5fcc-b1ef-4f5a0dca0429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bfd19a9-a685-5ea5-8c9a-dcf991921379" :question/math-academy-id "q-76564" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=4\\,i+7j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t[4\\,i+7j-(i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t(3i+5j) \\\\  & =(1+3t)\\,i+(2+5t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+3t$ and $y=2+5t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+5t)j \\\\  & =3i+5j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+3t)(2+5t) \\\\  & =15{t}^{2}+11t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{34}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{[5{t}^{3}+\\frac{11}{2}{t}^{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}(5+\\frac{11}{2}+2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d469bce-94af-5634-ab82-5aa6af354e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342616} {:answer/id #uuid "8209f70f-983a-5ae7-86d1-4e93d816e7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343353} {:answer/id #uuid "85c4d835-de5f-5323-ac7e-857e358d872b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343386} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d73c23-a898-521d-b2b3-a61c11d3d91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343697} {:answer/id #uuid "baa7a4d0-7ea9-5d62-9f82-07ada8a4ece3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8209f70f-983a-5ae7-86d1-4e93d816e7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343353} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b45262a-d4fd-5260-bbcc-f503f4fcadd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27ad0c20-f0d9-5a56-ac84-89e9373c74a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76569" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from$(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=2\\,i+j+3\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t[(2\\,i+j+3\\,k)-(i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t(i-j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+(2-t)\\,j+3t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t,$ $y=2-t,$ and $z=3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)k \\\\  & =i-j+3\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+t)(2-t)^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}+4t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}(\\frac{1}{4}-1+4)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9bcbed-0436-52a7-8a8d-e2de93c0b880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342362} {:answer/id #uuid "368a2103-e780-5582-ad6f-3a21aa3a8980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342688} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcbd128-6385-59fc-8240-9d369f5dd5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343182} {:answer/id #uuid "b163fa1b-99f8-590b-a1f2-94f8b252cb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343793} {:answer/id #uuid "bc47b99b-71b1-565f-a23d-6d3b20733bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e9bcbed-0436-52a7-8a8d-e2de93c0b880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342362} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed33cfa-1f9a-54f9-9d11-cd56d8af78bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07d5db28-c47d-5f6c-be75-b0e883a1921b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76577" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j+k$ and $b=i+2\\,j+5\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j+k)+t[(i+2\\,j+5\\,k)-(-i+2\\,j+k)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j+k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,k) \\\\  & =(-1+2t)\\,i+2\\,j+(1+4t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-1+2t,$ $y=2,$ and $z=1+4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+4t)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+0+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}((-1+2t)+(1+4t))) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0917ea-40c5-507b-8924-1137e4b9229d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342322} {:answer/id #uuid "28556a83-5674-5632-a7b0-74ac4c5a6211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342576} {:answer/id #uuid "356c398e-4bd2-59df-a741-dfdcb21b6a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{10}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342678} {:answer/id #uuid "5abb0cb1-003f-5e6f-b8df-beba529f73a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342996} {:answer/id #uuid "a44d1448-a188-5bd6-bad3-7888bafa60d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a0917ea-40c5-507b-8924-1137e4b9229d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342322} :answer-field/id #uuid "96bfae9f-8994-5c06-b70f-c7f375df05f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2db69cc9-2b8f-5c01-a877-0e37bcf27dc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76605" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+j$ and $b=2\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+j)+t[2\\,i+4\\,j-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(i+j)+t(i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+(1+3t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t$ and $y=1+3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)j \\\\  & =1\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi ((1+3t)-(1+t))}{8}) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{\\pi t}{4},$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b1571f-f5f7-562d-80a0-607aa3516530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342403} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9c1e54-997f-58a0-bd84-3b903059ab31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3-{cos}^{6}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342870} {:answer/id #uuid "7da69662-1fe5-58e3-b0c0-5897eb5c94d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343322} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd26a22-60c5-5518-ab97-c388fe07fef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344065} {:answer/id #uuid "d1097377-be4e-5b0d-8118-afb24f1f0f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da69662-1fe5-58e3-b0c0-5897eb5c94d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343322} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5e348e-6403-58e5-a33c-30c8b85d2e1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d123bbf4-7b8c-5b08-b206-9ffde691a39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76610" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-2{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5daa3e96-058a-548e-9727-56398515faab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343024} {:answer/id #uuid "65d6b8b8-c97c-5abc-aecb-7e85e4054fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343101} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb80056-6ab1-5d01-af98-8aaa5fd2fb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343301} {:answer/id #uuid "8a181e36-5fec-5c45-9657-66ff3657d6a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343440} {:answer/id #uuid "c39755ce-fc8d-515c-bacb-72a25ce9a67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c39755ce-fc8d-515c-bacb-72a25ce9a67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343959} :answer-field/id #uuid "87362288-9f29-50fe-9150-93bef3fb4a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5897420e-efe0-5c2e-8d46-6165bb9e5dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76616" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=2\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$  \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi /2)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05198b03-6b23-5849-aa82-9ea4c40d3eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342289} {:answer/id #uuid "2b813b05-cb54-5d88-a1d9-e1a9af16355e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342603} {:answer/id #uuid "36f33857-87bf-5be8-9076-60668d51b05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342690} {:answer/id #uuid "c2cf0438-4109-595c-84db-9d0fc3a246fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343954} {:answer/id #uuid "da0ecc33-5898-5828-a5c3-b8cb121ac86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b813b05-cb54-5d88-a1d9-e1a9af16355e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342603} :answer-field/id #uuid "9571d638-4cee-5239-b57d-7af35b79b119" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92df499c-114f-5a8f-9fd7-333656e7d6b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-76643" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9d0ea6-699e-529f-8386-4d8ae6eb9a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e\\sqrt{2}({e}^{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342592} {:answer/id #uuid "530ca827-8329-5ca0-99fc-22a829724023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342933} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e2be51-e86b-58ab-9a10-7841de2f4293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344151} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe6f6f8-75fd-55fd-9a9a-45090e8a563d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344202} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dafa4e-91ab-5e93-a865-b1e60c56b9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e2be51-e86b-58ab-9a10-7841de2f4293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344151} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3865ea-0943-5557-a486-c9bc91ea15eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2968de0-3f24-5e15-95ba-b17804d225c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-76650" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t[2\\,i+3\\,j-j] \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4(2t)+2){e}^{2t(1+2t)} \\\\  & =(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+4{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=(2+8t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aa8aaac-0518-5499-9551-f3a503a3343b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342594} {:answer/id #uuid "6e78a79c-9fcb-54b3-accd-8a3e6aece26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343187} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca7f4cc-2df8-563a-bc0b-51e94e0a3138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343620} {:answer/id #uuid "a8811e6b-48d4-50f6-ab5d-acec1fa73106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343721} {:answer/id #uuid "e82e3491-3796-5967-a4b3-1101c740da7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aa8aaac-0518-5499-9551-f3a503a3343b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342594} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc23f85e-8c81-57e2-8755-3ccd535824d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e85bae9c-7a72-56f0-9f05-0164e98daa66" :question/math-academy-id "q-76658" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(2y+3x)\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2y+3x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(0+3)-(-2+0) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6624c503-a440-59c9-8f2d-03632a1ac414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343105} {:answer/id #uuid "9a938235-b3f4-54c2-b79f-6fe77fb80b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343599} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd4d77b-6b5a-5089-99a1-e818c683648e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343625} {:answer/id #uuid "bb100409-5df2-5a5a-8228-1775440e2d46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343883} {:answer/id #uuid "d99f783f-0b69-5884-9e2f-48416231f2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dd4d77b-6b5a-5089-99a1-e818c683648e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343625} :answer-field/id #uuid "1748e2fb-ce8f-5df0-8345-b0b4c3f06cc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d7f3236-aee5-5d63-80b7-2ecd442664e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-76660" :question/problem "$6\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{7})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}) & =3\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})]\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7})} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a1cebb-a7ec-573e-834f-5765dcecf952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342394} {:answer/id #uuid "254c3227-d0aa-5723-a923-035c9df92844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342551} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab0592e-6a5f-5609-ad32-dc332fbcc425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343147} {:answer/id #uuid "83b4ab2f-a0ac-5b73-956c-314a45cef514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343363} {:answer/id #uuid "afff12a2-3582-5334-9126-b7ef6ab71ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83b4ab2f-a0ac-5b73-956c-314a45cef514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343363} :answer-field/id #uuid "24cdb73d-8bf4-5a1b-9d30-d95b06bf3c8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c41ea86a-3b09-5773-9843-df888de284a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76664" :question/problem "$6\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{10})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =3\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})]\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cee7c2d-32de-5ecb-b754-ad79568f9d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342741} {:answer/id #uuid "5fee99df-a2f2-53ad-aa42-aeb2b8227900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343052} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b8c0d-eb50-5a20-b648-4799432e59b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343321} {:answer/id #uuid "817d31a5-4314-5a33-8e83-382ed5fd5388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343350} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7f6c1c-7a72-538c-9708-f7cf3bcb5c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d7f6c1c-7a72-538c-9708-f7cf3bcb5c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343475} :answer-field/id #uuid "946d4e0c-fe04-5d7a-bb82-af9edb848223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a681b3bc-0951-5b58-83e9-55d23d5a0373" :question/math-academy-id "q-76688" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bba08e7-72f6-5628-a032-40fab3b1523b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342340} {:answer/id #uuid "966bb9f4-b258-530d-bda2-5cc45dd1bfc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343555} {:answer/id #uuid "da527ec0-d3e9-50cf-934b-d37d6ce14b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344156} {:answer/id #uuid "dc075c9d-4ff5-5e6a-83cc-7b9150d7aa65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344168} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3267aa-cefb-5be3-9449-fd0ab72c839c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc075c9d-4ff5-5e6a-83cc-7b9150d7aa65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344168} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b83e59-aa7d-5940-925e-575b2e63efa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee02b99-a9f3-5976-9c5b-34d5a43071ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-76690" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta | \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta |=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ because $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta >0.$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-0-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7eee1f5-4e67-5791-ab19-b18c7b3e839f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343993} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc4b0b3-3161-5573-8ff6-5f7ebe4c7119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344166} {:answer/id #uuid "e210fd40-0385-5c02-968e-6174892fbc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344228} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c07b9f-3789-507f-97de-8e306b0fb0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344384} {:answer/id #uuid "f5abf403-18d1-5fd6-8efd-732e33e8d35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3c07b9f-3789-507f-97de-8e306b0fb0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344384} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df0e8e5-c868-5ece-818b-a9164009ba5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d3824d0-05f9-53c5-8fe3-dac14910620a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76694" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{3}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5/2}-{0}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01aa8bda-9e61-569f-95fd-fa0673cb5508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342263} {:answer/id #uuid "04a7a2fb-7108-5b9a-b0f6-3ea038a2d3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342287} {:answer/id #uuid "0bbd5388-cd44-57ec-8a97-15388b8eeb75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342341} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0266d6-5dfc-5a4f-af26-5d7298ffde39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343151} {:answer/id #uuid "c742a1b7-bda6-5049-97fb-2691a979f098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b0266d6-5dfc-5a4f-af26-5d7298ffde39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343151} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4036142-1aad-5401-8af7-5636f44bca05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11d13a8c-3308-5126-9794-3bf56ac97de9" :question/math-academy-id "q-76736" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2=0?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{-\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/87/87b3a90142f4c810433bd2c219382379fc508fa426567708abddb744f39771c2.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x=\\theta +2n\\pi ,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "549eb3ff-0d70-5d98-95ae-140c946d9434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342942} {:answer/id #uuid "71f52d6b-9b86-50ff-8f7e-cfe37e69e6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343224} {:answer/id #uuid "9788f504-fc7f-5f00-9fe6-ff56fdf4863f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343567} {:answer/id #uuid "b6219bdb-a8f7-50e4-9cf6-285351b7c0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343834} {:answer/id #uuid "f41eacee-7b5a-5190-8daf-3e7d61906f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71f52d6b-9b86-50ff-8f7e-cfe37e69e6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343224} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c72e057-5521-52b8-8355-dcc75ede7d96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4b36406-0f67-50c9-b15a-12ee8329088e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76741" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}+1}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})=1+\\sqrt{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arctan}(\\sqrt{2}+1)=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi .$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b7fc9c2-4c69-54a4-80fc-bad3ecfa5ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342726} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8b986c-eacd-5892-87dd-71c0d9c6f4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342859} {:answer/id #uuid "9734c89e-bf7d-518a-b7d4-04a6d670adf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343564} {:answer/id #uuid "e6cf1f3f-478f-5826-b35f-1d2148451b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344263} {:answer/id #uuid "ffef2d32-f612-5254-bfaf-7abf41405410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffef2d32-f612-5254-bfaf-7abf41405410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344493} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c1cefb8-a187-5530-9672-96286f591e0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "84bd9e35-b1ad-5104-8c53-a77a665c8e52" :question/math-academy-id "q-76749" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}8{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =16{\\int }_{1}^{1/2}-{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-16\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}{|}_{1}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{6}({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}-{1}^{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}(-\\frac{63}{64}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db51a33-3a41-59c9-81e2-4977416f0bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342356} {:answer/id #uuid "4e66f044-704b-5e52-82fd-7775a345573b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342884} {:answer/id #uuid "611b54d2-27f5-5778-b31a-a615fefd7d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343061} {:answer/id #uuid "7ada0cf6-550a-5410-8925-e1587a2111ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343294} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3428c5-5eb7-5f6a-939d-3f6b641fe810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "611b54d2-27f5-5778-b31a-a615fefd7d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343061} :answer-field/id #uuid "8031f084-7386-5995-b791-a49b6fd8c3ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71319f17-956d-5226-b02f-5e0082742e93" :question/math-academy-id "q-7675" :question/problem "Express $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})=4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e1d1f28-5484-5c86-a90a-082235f872c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343482} {:answer/id #uuid "965afcff-952c-57f1-8381-d0d2c6cfa333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343554} {:answer/id #uuid "a4896ac8-ec47-5492-a067-e873101e9558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343690} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fcab50-4e3f-52f2-bbd9-053b07f4ba3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344075} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb4f5db-7a15-5a8d-92fa-4882f0394575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e1d1f28-5484-5c86-a90a-082235f872c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343482} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc4f513d-22f2-5838-ab63-68e9cd6ee512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20b5574b-0241-5d1f-8b06-4befe90938df" :question/math-academy-id "q-76765" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the polar curve $r=6$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =3({(3\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68904330-d856-56ab-ab89-95598aff7f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343132} {:answer/id #uuid "7643286e-f9ff-585d-9aca-43eda7f2b710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343260} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc1b583-ed2c-5e36-a6b5-501f45d92300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344043} {:answer/id #uuid "eba40804-2b69-5b03-8ebc-fcc8b5946e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi +1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344303} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2c9374-53c5-5786-8c80-f18bc786264b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccc1b583-ed2c-5e36-a6b5-501f45d92300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344043} :answer-field/id #uuid "e39c6e76-f79f-5404-bd14-1344e4f9c50e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21e9d3c4-49da-5fcb-92fe-c05185c24062" :question/math-academy-id "q-76767" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\theta  & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}-0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}+(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63bfa53b-e47b-50cc-89fd-5a4e4528fce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343084} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea6bc3-b230-56b5-bb39-3960352c7365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343288} {:answer/id #uuid "87e6f26e-9f09-5844-8065-ef122df54dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343414} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0991c3-27a4-50a9-8460-379ec4e4da59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343628} {:answer/id #uuid "d6cbc97b-38b5-54a3-92bc-84bf1dbbd503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6cbc97b-38b5-54a3-92bc-84bf1dbbd503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344123} :answer-field/id #uuid "60342b90-65d0-553f-8346-360744bd9458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccf4d567-d105-5c9b-9e19-604b98b9eea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76908" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2t,\\,2{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}+{(2{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1+2{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =1+2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+2{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(t+\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3+18-0 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f74f6bc-5e7b-58d7-b7fc-c365ef4e92f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342896} {:answer/id #uuid "57499e39-7bdc-59ad-a99d-360f1fc7b2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342966} {:answer/id #uuid "6677667e-3442-5a82-af63-c15f1638bd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343106} {:answer/id #uuid "fd321b38-e06d-5b93-9ae1-a114b299ba87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344462} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf30511-d971-58e6-a9d7-f63d74b0483c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6677667e-3442-5a82-af63-c15f1638bd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343106} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7fb0837-5c09-5cad-8bec-dfcdb3722f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f089db80-ac84-508f-a172-79a9d0b01dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76914" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2},\\,2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,3t,\\,4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+(3t)^{2}+{(4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡2t+9{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+9{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417d6dbd-2ce0-543a-b867-66f46ac5a28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342775} {:answer/id #uuid "4af00b12-c488-528b-8d8e-a83c42ba6f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342854} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6338b2-dd43-5df9-9664-ecfbf4ec9d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343153} {:answer/id #uuid "e29b77b4-101f-5781-976e-2efc7b315a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344231} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e5b481-08c2-5c6e-93f4-60c15385b21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4af00b12-c488-528b-8d8e-a83c42ba6f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342854} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63e4bae-b451-51c9-adf9-3b579789034f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6edd20b3-8235-5d98-a615-bc507b0983b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-77070" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6de928031610e2aaf55e3dd753449f766e7dc028a6618087e7489224a73ea7d.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8dbb58-a4a6-55f5-812f-bbbadd49ac81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342325} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecf715e-9eb9-5c8c-987e-7ff268923749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342757} {:answer/id #uuid "8a59df14-b3ee-5642-af36-7ee2d103d340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343441} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd0b2bd-4dc1-5624-8427-1ee555d53d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344064} {:answer/id #uuid "eb75c331-6648-59c4-aad8-3169be3d0cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a59df14-b3ee-5642-af36-7ee2d103d340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343441} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fb8bc68-6bb6-5b42-985f-e0bc0dd5f9eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d8b32ba-804f-5c40-bd42-e9f8325f480b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77159" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(2,4,1),$ $Q(3,4,2)$ and $R(3,3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P,$ $Q$ and $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,4,1⟩,\\,q=⟨3,4,2⟩,\\,r=⟨3,3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨3,3,1⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e6e668-b82c-5f64-b7e7-a49b270c95a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342251} {:answer/id #uuid "3869e1bb-0ad4-52cb-a463-44a68621aede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342702} {:answer/id #uuid "a3adb946-e512-55eb-a187-ced61f86a274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{65}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343679} {:answer/id #uuid "c10fdcde-262c-592d-97e1-748297e33ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343934} {:answer/id #uuid "d0600b8a-b989-5d6f-b12e-8260c9aaac01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c10fdcde-262c-592d-97e1-748297e33ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343934} :answer-field/id #uuid "cce7cda2-b89f-5700-a5ff-e0aac43f4d18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5e4f513-9065-59e2-89d1-49300cc3f2d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-77164" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(3t,t,4),$ $B(3t,4t,3)$ and $C(5t,t+1,5).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨3t,\\,t,\\,4⟩,\\,b=⟨3t,\\,4t,\\,3⟩,\\,c=⟨5t,\\,t+1,\\,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3t,\\,4t,\\,3⟩-⟨3t,\\,t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,3t,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨5t,\\,t+1,\\,5⟩-⟨3t,\\,t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=2$, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =2 \\\\ 0\\cdot 2t+3t\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1 & =2 \\\\ 3t-1 & =2 \\\\ 3t & =3 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,3,-1⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}})≈{75}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c58c77e-711b-5243-8324-c45babc5ec1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{64.52}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342349} {:answer/id #uuid "127b820a-d727-5cc4-ad0d-00f9acaa9c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{78.46}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342401} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa2bad3-5d98-52c1-be34-70c0e607bb74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{53.13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342762} {:answer/id #uuid "648602ed-37dc-531d-b1b7-9a5f65391672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{84.26}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343091} {:answer/id #uuid "905ab81d-e815-5d10-a042-a1138cbdb0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{66.42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "905ab81d-e815-5d10-a042-a1138cbdb0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{66.42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343500} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e4815b-d5b5-5483-9262-f030b9ac8c31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0fa3e2ff-f6be-5511-8653-2c146ace0ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-77165" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(5,3t+2,2),$ $B(1,3t,2)$ and $C(1,4t,3).$ Find $∠ABC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}=4.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨5,\\,3t+2,\\,2⟩,\\,b=⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩,\\,c=⟨1,\\,4t,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠ABC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/2c/2c81d48afacc4ac5e81ab501bc713009ea4425451fcf8dbf414672aeadbb1679.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =a-b \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,3t+2,\\,2⟩-⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =c-b \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,4t,\\,3⟩-⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,t,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}=4$, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =4 \\\\ 4\\cdot 0+2\\cdot t+0\\cdot 1 & =4 \\\\ 0+2t+0 & =4 \\\\ 2t & =4 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =⟨4,2,0⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{BA}||\\overset{\\to }{BC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})≈{66.42}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076d4f2b-e206-5510-92ce-4af922c0cfe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{21.42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342302} {:answer/id #uuid "09c10234-682a-51c0-848d-206b08c9c6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10.52}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342319} {:answer/id #uuid "65b42fb4-c57a-5acb-8b41-1a6bc8e50526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77.07}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343100} {:answer/id #uuid "8da02dc4-e6c4-5060-a581-992098de8461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63.43}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343477} {:answer/id #uuid "8f319fd7-e716-51df-a6fb-e0a06bd1cd90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{26.57}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f319fd7-e716-51df-a6fb-e0a06bd1cd90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{26.57}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343492} :answer-field/id #uuid "453dd8cb-e3c7-57eb-bb1b-6f649dd1458a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2aca6a5f-5821-5a6c-aed6-f1272cbcca9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-77167" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(2t,4,3),$ $B(5t-1,4,3)$ and $C(2t+4,t+5,3).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=8.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2t,\\,4,\\,3⟩,\\,b=⟨5t-1,\\,4,\\,3⟩,\\,c=⟨2t+4,\\,t+5,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨5t-1,\\,4,\\,3⟩-⟨2t,\\,4,\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2t+4,\\,t+5,\\,3⟩-⟨2t,\\,4,\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,t+1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=8,$ we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =8 \\\\ (3t-1)\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (t+1)+0\\cdot 0 & =8 \\\\ 4(3t-1)+0+0 & =8 \\\\ 12t-4 & =8 \\\\ 12t & =12 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0,0⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{80}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})≈{26.57}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ccbd978-8163-5c7e-baae-b8ad95985a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342350} {:answer/id #uuid "453e49ec-25af-50ef-847c-f9e8f100fa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342807} {:answer/id #uuid "78d2918a-06f4-5e2f-8f95-8ce2e0a1d53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343281} {:answer/id #uuid "7c924cb2-9540-5923-aef1-54902d61b6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343312} {:answer/id #uuid "a953ff99-deba-55f2-9b54-5b5b5650e514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ccbd978-8163-5c7e-baae-b8ad95985a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342350} :answer-field/id #uuid "10c5cc9a-ab7f-5bd2-8f67-b0bc3b91ae44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fc56ee5-fc5d-5429-bfdb-4254f7ee30fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-77191" :question/problem "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of the line segment from the point $B(0,1)$ to the point $D(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\frac{3}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x-2y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨-2,0⟩+t(⟨0,1⟩-⟨-2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨-2+2t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(-2+2t)i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=-2+2t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =(x-2y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j \\\\  & =((-2+2t)-2t)\\,i+(t-(-2+2t))\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+(2-t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(-2\\,i+(2-t)\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =-2-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+(-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2-t)\\,\\text{d}t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & ={[-2t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =[-2(1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0]-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e01622d-5238-571c-86a2-cc69aea35c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342879} {:answer/id #uuid "7d299b65-f394-5c5c-bc63-8fb5e73724a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343315} {:answer/id #uuid "871eb9c5-4cb7-5c05-bdc5-c74d2fbd75af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343406} {:answer/id #uuid "8d30a21f-b968-57af-b44c-c417188aad45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343472} {:answer/id #uuid "ff09f4de-ca16-5c7e-bd14-73c99669c280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d30a21f-b968-57af-b44c-c417188aad45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343472} :answer-field/id #uuid "329c69e0-19d4-5553-979b-455f51b68eba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4727f73-629e-56a3-9ba0-48f8c8b86b89" :question/math-academy-id "q-77237" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x.$ \n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={300}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={300}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/5f/5fe064cdc2d97e3db894801f6f31a4679881efcf83564e24a1ea2559c15d2085.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={300}^{∘},{240}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=300\\cdot 240=72\\,000.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d6e8b6-ad5f-5464-aba0-522f12866aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76\\,432" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342780} {:answer/id #uuid "5afa29e4-ff89-5b64-8604-e11d59ded96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67\\,513" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342997} {:answer/id #uuid "806fad21-7cc6-580e-b162-1aa2eaeedb2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52\\,364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343344} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccb1e7f-2396-5c8f-9cb8-1ec4acaeb448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86\\,234" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343469} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b26a8f-3fd3-5c46-96ac-ca609f137f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5afa29e4-ff89-5b64-8604-e11d59ded96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67\\,513" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342997} :answer-field/id #uuid "9094753f-7e5d-5c0d-80b3-3c348c13e8f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50313fbe-b38c-5ce8-ba23-7557d9ba1d58" :question/math-academy-id "q-77321" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{2}{7})=(-16.601\\,5…)^{∘}≈-{16.602}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{16.602}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={343.398}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={343.398}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={16.602}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={16.602}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/c8/c86fca87f7f46c0155be358f377550a4adebf1a2040c874d55c00473766745f5.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{16.602}^{∘} \\\\  & ={196.602}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈343.398\\cdot 196.602=67\\,513\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48d7d4f9-cc8a-581c-be35-d8f2ce13846a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{4}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342837} {:answer/id #uuid "85e5795d-b19e-5643-a38f-a0da7b3d6d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\,j-\\frac{1}{4}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343388} {:answer/id #uuid "bbafed32-dd0f-5c01-9a8e-7410fedcd53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,j-\\frac{1}{4}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343887} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc8dac-5428-55ea-966a-6e8f824e3ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344012} {:answer/id #uuid "ceaf2a04-fd6e-55b0-8db0-5b009d339897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e5795d-b19e-5643-a38f-a0da7b3d6d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\,j-\\frac{1}{4}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343388} :answer-field/id #uuid "232e97df-a9bc-5885-b743-1f8a9e12529e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a91bca78-5c50-5527-ac8e-ebf8cd03d30a" :question/math-academy-id "q-77678" :question/problem "Find the derivative of  $f(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-5t)\\,j+\\frac{1}{1+t}\\,k$ at $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-5t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{1+t})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,i+(6t-5)\\,j-\\frac{1}{(1+t)^{2}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(1)$ we substitute $t=1$ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{1+(1)^{2}}\\,i+(6(1)-5)\\,j-\\frac{1}{(1+(1))^{2}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\,j-\\frac{1}{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dcf0bbd-b192-5add-9e84-0c3a55f950ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343025} {:answer/id #uuid "abd5f7d7-9ada-5435-8924-b543a4cefa50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343750} {:answer/id #uuid "b68d64a3-a2cf-57ce-a02e-92106f2c76c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343838} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a6b3ed-28bc-53b9-8b87-9aefb92707ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344210} {:answer/id #uuid "f81047c8-bb32-5999-8d1f-b2f474d0c494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b68d64a3-a2cf-57ce-a02e-92106f2c76c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343838} :answer-field/id #uuid "a682acb0-9e3c-56e0-b472-9929a73cfafb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f86a415f-799e-539e-80b6-365c16f597c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-77696" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{12}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{5}{12})=-0.429\\,775…≈-0.429\\,8\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-0.429\\,8+2\\pi =5.853\\,385…≈5.853\\,4\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.429\\,8.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.429\\,8.$\n\n![](images/91/912225edc2b860674da7531597fc905a1a9c821eda705dbdcfafdce32385839b.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.429\\,8 \\\\  & ≈3.571\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈5.853\\,4\\cdot 3.571\\,4=20.9\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5882ab-8d08-5d69-a3a1-ae020f06abdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342634} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea32b5e-b3cb-59c4-9540-cb5750845fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342756} {:answer/id #uuid "a33ba453-64f9-517d-a9e4-a0dc1c64be02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343671} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c4a6d7-7b5c-5162-aea4-2a9e331bd48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344225} {:answer/id #uuid "f4553fd9-482e-57f9-8fdb-47bc7b83b05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a33ba453-64f9-517d-a9e4-a0dc1c64be02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343671} :answer-field/id #uuid "03fd1df5-b4a2-528c-93e5-2adb0a05c267" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b4cd109-3045-57f0-ac19-cffa3906a774" :question/math-academy-id "q-77788" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies in the required domain, the first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/a4/a45a91dfd90e1fe477d562486d641e4932722ca62645c3eb4024c4f851c964d3.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ae6a06-4330-54ef-9433-c5c677530ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342293} {:answer/id #uuid "1d848a19-5850-569d-891e-af12d31cce5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342484} {:answer/id #uuid "5735c8bc-a5bf-5456-8e38-dd27a7af277f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342964} {:answer/id #uuid "765b12b0-0b4e-5056-b17d-e6c391b48100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343263} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef93109-94c1-5adc-8254-1c56bb5d8e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{\\pi }^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "765b12b0-0b4e-5056-b17d-e6c391b48100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343263} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6b87d1f-4463-5d15-83fa-d1424a0990e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eedaeea2-0b11-5fde-945c-2d7775198c4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-77789" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies in the required domain, the first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/00/00d540b1f6412e4af2617452fbf0c58750d01e844d89d0d92cfacc0cb3e45e32.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1085f982-102e-5f07-a1af-72eb7d1c34e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342382} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3ae0ad-af44-5806-a122-a092b83919b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342471} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0e67b5-1194-5c31-882d-e9e2b05641f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342734} {:answer/id #uuid "52c201ef-70f3-581a-ac26-30a85642f599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{\\pi }^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342930} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a87ff2-b7a9-51e9-a4fd-3947c63ff974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1085f982-102e-5f07-a1af-72eb7d1c34e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342382} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e352b29-38a7-57d1-a3d3-beac88dbcd42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "228c716c-ab14-5643-95bb-25368bb13b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-77804" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-2$ for $-2\\pi \\le x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/85/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ is not in the required domain $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})(-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0100a6cf-26e4-55e2-a991-641df9a44bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342254} {:answer/id #uuid "39e2b888-25bd-532c-bc70-aec5890e1462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342716} {:answer/id #uuid "52dfc24b-4779-5617-a5bc-7d5bd4291cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342932} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ae7fa3-f268-5452-85e7-60bee4fc74c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343680} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5bb6cb-5539-5713-b68b-af5f7248ead3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3ae7fa3-f268-5452-85e7-60bee4fc74c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343680} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa859cfd-da77-5ee0-90e2-b40c9ab9f852" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e3950f8-1078-57cf-a1d0-199da15e87ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-77805" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/2e/2e1f1ef6c82e30b5f9a591fca432914562f949a7ed5cabc3c4eb92bfecf899ee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ is not in the required domain $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "117be22f-0498-56c8-8c5e-eeea3bfa919b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345500} {:answer/id #uuid "42848b2b-0583-5a3a-b1e5-791f99f8dd1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345921} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddd3c81-6a6b-5d64-914b-025b09f1a30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346452} {:answer/id #uuid "e23f104d-f3a1-52e8-b59e-42a5c19f1d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347341} {:answer/id #uuid "f446b28c-37f3-5de6-9491-dc3048430119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f446b28c-37f3-5de6-9491-dc3048430119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347519} :answer-field/id #uuid "abaa5846-1c43-5a58-ae01-c2d971b74ea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46a07c22-0617-5695-b7be-c28f6615ea99" :question/math-academy-id "q-78013" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}+5}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{2}+5}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{(\\frac{2x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+\\frac{5}{x})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+0)}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0deca599-ab0e-52b8-9014-d10ec63b0b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,s}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345462} {:answer/id #uuid "23e518c4-aa56-56d4-ac96-d8de1b630937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{8}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345680} {:answer/id #uuid "27597662-d72b-58c7-9c73-6d7aa227dbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{4},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{4},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{8}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345705} {:answer/id #uuid "cee297c6-6c2a-579f-97ba-46b06d834946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,s}{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,s}{6}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347163} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fdf0bf-0fdc-570c-aed5-7c3e6ec100e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0fdf0bf-0fdc-570c-aed5-7c3e6ec100e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347327} :answer-field/id #uuid "329b56ad-7508-5224-a7c6-7d7ef0019f7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec5db497-36ab-565c-9851-a44645728973" :question/math-academy-id "q-78390" :question/problem "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},\\,\\frac{t}{2},\\,t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\cdot u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,t$,  we have that $t=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s,\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c6e8a62-6c5e-5c07-8b38-9629f6e61c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\frac{6}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346290} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc9ddc0-286e-5730-8348-630a2c6bfe50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346297} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9bcc86-1dfb-5e38-8fa0-9abbc2ce5363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\frac{3}{7},\\,\\frac{6}{7}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346701} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5c653c-10ca-5639-86e6-15c284b3313c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{14},\\,\\frac{3}{14},\\,\\frac{2}{7}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347148} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ec4efb-ec1e-5eee-8d21-72d1fcfd2de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{7},\\,\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\frac{3}{7}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a9bcc86-1dfb-5e38-8fa0-9abbc2ce5363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\frac{3}{7},\\,\\frac{6}{7}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346701} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e2f562-fc1a-5512-a817-ac49a9aa2b67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80d24151-001d-558c-b4cf-13fe29c86835" :question/math-academy-id "q-78403" :question/problem "Given the curve  $r(t)=⟨2{e}^{t},\\,3{e}^{t},\\,6{e}^{t}⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=7({e}^{t}-1),$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=7({e}^{t}-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{s}{7} & ={e}^{t}-1 \\\\ {e}^{t} & =\\frac{s}{7}+1 \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})) \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})},\\,3{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})},\\,6{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7},\\,3\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7},\\,6\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2s+14}{7},\\,\\frac{3s+21}{7},\\,\\frac{6s+42}{7}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2s+14}{7}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3s+21}{7}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{6s+42}{7})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\frac{3}{7},\\,\\frac{6}{7}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a27ae7-7c57-54e7-b03e-9fbdacd374c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346118} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf92d0c-6f00-5421-9e59-486b9104a848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346136} {:answer/id #uuid "848cef78-b991-5da0-869f-d697a30cac16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346507} {:answer/id #uuid "a478cb86-21b0-5f14-9d49-520709495b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346780} {:answer/id #uuid "abfbffad-dd3b-50fa-9486-91574e2fb7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abfbffad-dd3b-50fa-9486-91574e2fb7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346852} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d14c1f-bb28-5566-b0d5-5be361b027e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99e56538-c134-5d33-a767-559703eb3b5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78490" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}})}^{n}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "We see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{64}{{2}^{k}} & <1 \\\\ 64 & <{2}^{k} \\\\ {2}^{k} & >64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${2}^{k}=64$ has the solution $k=6.$ Now, since ${2}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${2}^{k}>64$ must be $k>6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "688a94f7-68ae-5aea-b884-bdf89550bcc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346259} {:answer/id #uuid "71e69b0b-7def-5650-8e27-9bb24af7e90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346359} {:answer/id #uuid "892e1109-ff79-534d-a849-855beda0ff40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346541} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9e3d56-23b9-521e-b8ae-895d4452cf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346573} {:answer/id #uuid "f0718dd5-540c-5350-bb5b-ba0be70bea46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c9e3d56-23b9-521e-b8ae-895d4452cf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346573} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c72b5e4-f49c-5a8b-a72b-54dbc4394d47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9ab6aec-b415-5906-90bf-de072de6dfbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-78891" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a45999ec28d309b3be249304e9b5175f66f2b862cd7f18f2c51b3182333e9a08.png)\n\nFind the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that we want to find the area between the curve $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9690a7-a7e2-5397-9fab-5932d5f57697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345480} {:answer/id #uuid "259386fa-930c-5ed2-8146-0410d65c7c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345690} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1db9ec-13d0-523f-beab-84f5738731bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345876} {:answer/id #uuid "d42af497-46f2-5618-b60b-d642f0f7e4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347206} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d750ad-e2fd-5a5b-a423-66b337485f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d750ad-e2fd-5a5b-a423-66b337485f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6288fe0-74a5-5333-b0d5-8d242026b44c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9131a7ff-7e61-5a90-96bc-6b1cab75b7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-7892" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{\\pi },\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-P{e}^{\\pi } & ={e}^{\\pi } \\\\ P & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{2x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2P{e}^{\\pi }-2Q{e}^{\\pi } & =4{e}^{\\pi } \\\\ -2P-2Q & =4 \\\\ 2-2Q & =4 \\\\ Q & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-1$ and $Q=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39693a15-ca73-520c-96b8-70bf5c7d2bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345840} {:answer/id #uuid "560dffe9-bd15-5874-987a-19875f37b61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346081} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1c8d3d-9216-563e-89f4-57f9cd94617d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346721} {:answer/id #uuid "e826709d-b840-5266-9a60-3cdfdb2ab7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347397} {:answer/id #uuid "e9675bf4-65aa-5c5c-937a-f453f7376542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e826709d-b840-5266-9a60-3cdfdb2ab7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347397} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a0768cb-efe0-570d-bfc5-96d892680cad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7aa8476b-0bf3-5524-88ba-54ab4c70f153" :question/math-academy-id "q-78947" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{3}-1,\\,y=3{t}^{6}+2$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=18{t}^{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{18{t}^{5}}{3{t}^{2}}=6{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(6{t}^{3})}{3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18{t}^{2}}{3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ad9106-6449-5425-9ea8-8c4d982508a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345373} {:answer/id #uuid "45dc42bc-9e4d-55b0-9f61-b8cbcec2623e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345960} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9b883-5db0-5670-b24b-baadb64f5beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346787} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4fe63e-62d3-5e7b-87ef-17e62dfe7c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346988} {:answer/id #uuid "e4746b2e-d4c3-51e0-b47c-2a5f28dfd1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9b883-5db0-5670-b24b-baadb64f5beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346787} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bbb2f12-0ff8-5ad2-898f-75f1bc5be3b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "858cfde7-5900-59e1-9b39-5d78acc22281" :question/math-academy-id "q-78950" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t))}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b139ee-f463-5a47-9c6a-a41512770e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345503} {:answer/id #uuid "19031ec8-e1ff-542a-a61e-a82e8669c57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345572} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3696a9-c1f5-5688-a685-20e48aa47576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345625} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a736b8-6f9b-5cac-8168-604e09548620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347219} {:answer/id #uuid "ee140afe-fb06-5d83-b607-8a7268797ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee140afe-fb06-5d83-b607-8a7268797ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347453} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce54c950-38e1-57f1-9dbf-c1bfdc34311a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57251548-96ca-57aa-b00f-0da810dd66ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-78951" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{csc}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-2{csc}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bbf0b70-eb73-5737-870e-2b2a733a604d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345593} {:answer/id #uuid "22ea9738-c711-5104-a068-657f18af36ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345664} {:answer/id #uuid "58320d87-d5f1-5831-b7ba-1fc8e51312dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346104} {:answer/id #uuid "7d80556e-d68c-5841-8c9b-4fac2591689a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346449} {:answer/id #uuid "de267612-3488-5cfc-bf84-8951e79d0127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d80556e-d68c-5841-8c9b-4fac2591689a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346449} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb795487-a850-5ed5-84bc-cdf04a7dfdc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2921b965-ad16-58ab-8156-5829abb484a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-78954" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-2,\\,y={t}^{7}+2.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=7{t}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=7{t}^{2},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-2)={t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(7{t}^{2})}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14t}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=2}=\\frac{14}{(2)^{3}}=\\frac{14}{8}=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3809a2c4-e286-5533-8268-56808f38ebc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345832} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8a55e4-0e06-547f-9596-7d385a8aca59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346000} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4fc52a-cffd-56af-b644-7097c7369df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346987} {:answer/id #uuid "d01a593e-4629-5e99-9d9a-1fdcdbeaad33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347172} {:answer/id #uuid "fd366b29-1ac9-50f1-8b03-6832bb98e998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc4fc52a-cffd-56af-b644-7097c7369df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346987} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f1d1df-1c69-5787-a1d5-743287fa0870" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65b71b88-3c0c-5738-9071-c6f9c6dd22d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-78955" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{4}-5,\\,y=4{t}^{5}+2.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=5t,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=5t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{4}-5)=4{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(5t)}{4{t}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=1}=\\frac{5}{4(1)^{3}}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "051310d8-27d9-55b7-9973-63ffa9cb14aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-3{x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345392} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4113e3-0764-5672-9891-a633856b765a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345869} {:answer/id #uuid "ede3205e-3227-5dea-8169-7b24d8965679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347452} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a4f59c-0e70-5bb8-a82c-7d6417df818d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ede3205e-3227-5dea-8169-7b24d8965679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347452} :answer-field/id #uuid "482202fe-3be8-520f-b799-223ef4007503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c32c46c2-ed3e-5d40-a351-d00d1deae676" :question/math-academy-id "q-78957" :question/problem "$\\int 9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=9{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 9{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=3{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{3}-\\int 3{x}^{3}(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{x}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de5e89c-9bab-5e31-91a2-df2583f9dc7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345874} {:answer/id #uuid "c5118d6c-cf93-505e-b96a-07d7ffa1abde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347057} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ab5c1f-7f45-58e8-a1d2-fc3564ff0157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347220} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4071a9-b517-5b62-8eaf-4c67a84085ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347427} {:answer/id #uuid "f477e0a7-c04a-5dbe-b975-87caea0b0232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f477e0a7-c04a-5dbe-b975-87caea0b0232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347520} :answer-field/id #uuid "76065987-93a6-5a30-bc13-15c05e80e811" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb80aff3-58bc-5e27-a3a9-2dbd0c5a0d01" :question/math-academy-id "q-78959" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+4,\\,y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t),$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t),$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+4)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t))}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{sec}^{2}⁡(3t)}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & =4{sec}^{3}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /9} & =4{sec}^{3}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f96a4f6-c7df-5b23-97fa-260cef9d68be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345628} {:answer/id #uuid "22cb1788-f857-5e59-aaab-779ce03b316f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345663} {:answer/id #uuid "353a0206-9de7-58c6-a562-3aed78a4d110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345810} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9c674c-51d9-5271-9f71-c996c7132e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346002} {:answer/id #uuid "c54919c3-b2a2-58ab-b93c-de494422252e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b9c674c-51d9-5271-9f71-c996c7132e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346002} :answer-field/id #uuid "8865fef7-a8ee-53c7-84bd-642905358225" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9aef34f7-3e6f-5d43-be54-6f8bf558b39a" :question/math-academy-id "q-78965" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{x})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{x})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{x}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{x}-\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2452d0e1-4362-5796-a78d-dd02a6585ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345683} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf12949-3c55-5003-9dd7-df558ba34624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346441} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f87ce7-dd80-5709-99d0-ca3b27881647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346896} {:answer/id #uuid "bbebdb1c-5559-5ac4-b7a7-c46f4dd61296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346983} {:answer/id #uuid "f0deae7a-65de-5629-be07-d8599eee87d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cf12949-3c55-5003-9dd7-df558ba34624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346441} :answer-field/id #uuid "8571455f-3add-56cd-b95f-65d5a2eb936b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83cb784b-d860-58fe-8b2f-7d0321406fb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79195" :question/problem "![](images/89/89efc20f1c6527d1bcd501f97ce585fb5a60e9791804f56d61f33653cf1a70d7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/f8/f8617da98ea9d9fb48b8fef588b624c6961a299233836fe433c1819b697ca0fb.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19979361-a8df-557d-82d0-af8fd1cd5d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438574} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c92711-50d6-5a72-8bfe-847ca17732fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1c92711-50d6-5a72-8bfe-847ca17732fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438575} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ef534af-2098-561e-b179-3f55b5991272" :answer-field/key "$y=\\frac{7}{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439316} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c1b3ff-9199-58c6-8074-ef77f3973076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438576} {:answer/id #uuid "81f1c637-5060-5374-898f-afcdf1cec8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81f1c637-5060-5374-898f-afcdf1cec8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438577} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac7782cf-bc1c-5aca-a517-f8894341953d" :answer-field/key "$y=-\\frac{1}{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439317} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c7481b3-4b8f-5e17-a81a-78de2657429f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438578} {:answer/id #uuid "b09ebf27-8594-5384-8496-cf3a363bb462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b09ebf27-8594-5384-8496-cf3a363bb462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438579} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b6785e8-bf84-599d-a8f6-c5925f0b9071" :answer-field/key "$y=\\frac{2}{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2809b1ba-af02-5b95-b4d7-d9885f873cd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79333" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{7}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{7}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{2}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=\\frac{7}{x}$; $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$; $y=\\frac{2}{x}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "451f3842-16b6-5866-9651-73784c8644e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{4}-x+2}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345950} {:answer/id #uuid "58be30d8-ef50-5ef7-b5f3-523380bbc11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346113} {:answer/id #uuid "70a616da-72b0-575c-adfa-c326848aeb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346340} {:answer/id #uuid "816eafe7-746e-5226-88e6-c6e7b7b43820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}-x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346482} {:answer/id #uuid "f1baeeb1-4f87-5895-9e67-a1a7e8844e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "816eafe7-746e-5226-88e6-c6e7b7b43820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}-x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346482} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea329cec-2c85-5a3c-ae6a-b47fc15cd1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49fb438b-3c89-5f2d-ad22-0703828a1cf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-79373" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-y}=4{x}^{3}{e}^{-y}$ that satisfies $y(1)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-y} & =4{x}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4{x}^{3}{e}^{-y}-{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(4{x}^{3}-1){e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4{x}^{3}-1 \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (4{x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & ={x}^{4}-x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}-x+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}-x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{4}-1+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡C & =0 \\\\ C & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-y}=4{x}^{3}{e}^{-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}-x+1).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a3ff9d8-d74e-5343-ad61-f38d75a4634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345581} {:answer/id #uuid "92bcda09-bbf9-5a09-9aed-4bbc2b18b5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346634} {:answer/id #uuid "a49ae4b9-9666-5b59-92ef-e09360fa069f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346782} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d4ed89-4c43-5075-8824-a4e4e5a63675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346946} {:answer/id #uuid "cce46d7b-2b95-5322-b973-14b997e8b227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce46d7b-2b95-5322-b973-14b997e8b227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347146} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c86181e-c545-5faa-804b-35aa0f7d0b42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8375e591-b238-583f-a781-920a1106c021" :question/math-academy-id "q-79581" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{3}-(tx)^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{3}{y}^{3}-{t}^{3}{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{3}({y}^{3}-{x}^{3})}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y)={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2df498d-24df-552d-9963-ba92a60720c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347036} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ec428-3fb3-58c3-b004-b0f150228d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=(2{e}^{-\\pi }+2-\\pi )\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347127} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5e4828-9fa1-5e5f-a596-b6a0941bae2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=(2{e}^{-\\pi /2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347139} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e23759-83d8-5271-bc42-5538170c9e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347416} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc85d07-20f1-5e9b-929a-e9e726b49cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=(2{e}^{-\\pi }+1-\\pi )\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2df498d-24df-552d-9963-ba92a60720c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347036} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4cca97e-d94f-5e5a-89e5-a4edd41ea002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e75039a0-8210-5276-92d0-934efa0fce84" :question/math-academy-id "q-79600" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=8-16t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at $y=2\\,\\text{m}$ with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2-2t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+2-2t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at $y=2\\,\\text{m}$ with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=2$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2-2t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{-2(\\pi /4)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+2-2(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49f46a15-0ca3-5841-b2b5-75e0e85c81df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345982} {:answer/id #uuid "60b84d6c-d8f3-52d7-8796-88e3110d630d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346189} {:answer/id #uuid "adbb7b5a-753f-5d50-9dee-99ba55ca3aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346866} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aa1892-d783-5d98-8e84-8e8aa43ec658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346908} {:answer/id #uuid "b54dbd62-5d7e-5ae5-8499-c4d6f981662d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2aa1892-d783-5d98-8e84-8e8aa43ec658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346908} :answer-field/id #uuid "e68a9f18-b7c7-5562-af22-77d1084739c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e13f24b1-d2d1-59a7-beb7-3c206203362c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79607" :question/problem "Given that $D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=-1,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0646fdce-fd3c-56a7-8137-632d215be116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345404} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9afd84-fe8c-56a0-89e1-00f6ac764479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(1+x)y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346123} {:answer/id #uuid "71040d28-62bb-5d78-b4e6-2a190574112e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346349} {:answer/id #uuid "d204e6a5-02d7-5404-bddf-a96974b0383d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347190} {:answer/id #uuid "d895b1af-8150-5cb7-9f48-017f2ef90f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71040d28-62bb-5d78-b4e6-2a190574112e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346349} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ddf3aa-733e-5972-8973-46d76ff540e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f284d9d4-47c8-54c3-aa2b-11e7ba22d40f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79697" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{x+y}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}+xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+\\underbrace{(-x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{{x}^{2}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02dad359-25f2-59b1-a326-992f7d8f2be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345374} {:answer/id #uuid "37a0854b-5ffd-53b5-a97e-3fc8c049dafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345828} {:answer/id #uuid "78a766e2-5151-5cd4-bd3b-cdc71e40524c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346407} {:answer/id #uuid "9d887fd2-2962-51ac-ad91-2c074402c2fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346722} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcdcfbb-2ee8-5177-94d0-d8a8b2bddfea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}x+(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37a0854b-5ffd-53b5-a97e-3fc8c049dafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345828} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ddc30c-7b7f-5181-82b4-b214c29b463f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af353571-e5f3-5cb8-87db-52892f4fbe38" :question/math-academy-id "q-79739" :question/problem "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{x}^{3}y-{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05905e56-e689-5126-840d-bc1116dd72e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+12{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345402} {:answer/id #uuid "67caff4a-7e36-5bec-ac58-0c42c8671613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}+12{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346257} {:answer/id #uuid "8cab1644-ef8d-51ab-a9c3-417c7b7e3cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x}{x}+4{y}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346574} {:answer/id #uuid "9a950f20-f785-50c1-a237-5e98fddf7f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+12{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346700} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8fc4ec-515e-5142-8b79-f41f0428a75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{x}+12{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac8fc4ec-515e-5142-8b79-f41f0428a75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{x}+12{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346855} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ffa6a66-235b-5631-a0c1-500618323098" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08d66e91-862a-530e-b18f-86f79ced0d5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79740" :question/problem "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)=4{y}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{x}+12{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d612bc-dd52-5831-b337-fa6defcd96f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345513} {:answer/id #uuid "564d124b-ce3d-51b6-ae90-cc269fdd3e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\text{d}x+x\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346083} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb6539d-0734-51a3-a90e-aa8418df3be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346601} {:answer/id #uuid "bc642f54-a808-5aa1-8693-2bd65ddb7d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\text{d}x+{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346989} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b57932-9ec9-52df-820f-e563f00d3236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1b57932-9ec9-52df-820f-e563f00d3236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347489} :answer-field/id #uuid "3670839f-8614-5e81-9b1f-cfef898